re subject to change without notice\n\n## UP_Mathematics and AMS Catalogue [Link] 23 24-11-2025 16:00:46\n\n24\nMathematics [Link]\n\n## 2025 384 pp Paperback\n\nAnalysis\n978-93-49750-84-5 ` 1520.00\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElliptic Curves, Modular Forms, and Their L-functions | Mathematical Association of America (https://old.maa.org/press/maa-reviews/elliptic-curves-modular-forms-and-their-l-functions)\nciteturn571search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.6 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; The three foci of the book under review are, as the title indicates, elliptic curves, modular functions, and L-functions; indeed, Lozano-Robledo stresses their interconnectedness, and it can’t be otherwise in the wake of, e.g., Wiles’ conquest of Fermat’s Last Theorem. ... Riemann’s great monograph of 1859, Über die Anzahl der Primzahlen unter einer gegebenen Grösse, containing his approach to the Prime Number Theorem as well as the statement of his Hypothesis about the zeta function, already addresses the interplay between half-integral weight modular forms (theta functions) and Dirichlet series (like the zeta function), for example. ... Of course, the themes Lozano-Robledo addresses in Elliptic Curves, Modular Forms, and Their L-Functions are deep and sophisticated; considerable mathematical background is required for their mastery.\n\n# Elliptic Curves, Modular Forms, and Their L-functions\n\nImage\n\n###### \n\nÁlvaro Lozano-Robledo\n\nPublisher: \n\nAmerican Mathematical Society/Institute for Advance Study\n\nPublication Date: \n\n2011\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n195\n\nFormat: \n\nPaperback\n\nSeries: \n\nStudent Mathematical Library 58\n\nPrice: \n\n37.00\n\nISBN: \n\n9780821852422\n\nCategory: \n\nMonograph\n\n  * MAA Review\n  * Table of Contents\n\n[Reviewed by\n\nMichael Berg\n\n, on\n\n03/24/2011\n\n]\n\nI think this is a wonderful book.\n\nIn less than 200 pages Lozano-Robledo covers a solid amount of modern number theory in a manner altogether accessible to a novice, and in a fashion so as to convey number theory’s irresistible beauty, due both to its connections to very simply phrased deep arithmetical questions (e.g. the behavior of congruent numbers) and its use of stunning techniques (such as the yoga of L-functions).\n\nThe three foci of the book under review are, as the title indicates, elliptic curves, modular functions, and L-functions; indeed, Lozano-Robledo stresses their interconnectedness, and it can’t be otherwise in the wake of, e.g., Wiles’ conquest of Fermat’s Last Theorem.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMATH 5020 - The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves | Álvaro Lozano-Robledo | Department of Mathematics | University of Connecticut (https://alozano.clas.uconn.edu/math5020f15/)\nciteturn571search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Milne, “Elliptic Curves”, Kea Books – this book is freely available at Milne’s website. ... Koblitz, “Introduction to Elliptic Curves and Modular Forms”. ... L. ... Silverman’s book contains an excellent collection of exercises. ... 7  | 4  | 1, 2, 3  | Formal groups, expansion around O, and groups associated to formal groups  | Riemann-Roch and Weierstrass models\n\n  6. D. Husemöller, “Elliptic Curves“, Second Edition, Springer.\n\n  7. L. Washington, “Elliptic Curves: Number Theory and Cryptography“, Second Edition (Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications) 2nd Edition, CRC Press.\n\n## Homework\n\nDuring the semester I will propose assignments to be handed in or presented in class. Silverman’s book contains an excellent collection of exercises. I will also propose exercises to be solved with Magma (or Sage, or PARI). You are encouraged to talk with your classmates about the homework. If you have difficulties, do not waste time — get help! Please come to office hours!\nHomework assignments:\n\n  * Homework 1 (due Tuesday, September 15th) — 1.3, 1.6. In addition: find a 1-parameter parametrization of all the rational points on the conic x^2-3xy+y^2=5. Also, problems 2.3, 2.6.\n  * Homework 2.\n\n## A tentative schedule, day by day\n\nDay  | Ch.  | Sec.  | Topics (ideally)  | Topics (reality)\n1  |    |  | Introduction, Algebraic Curves  | Introduction to Diophantine Equations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNumber Theory and Geometry | Álvaro Lozano-Robledo | Department of Mathematics | University of Connecticut (https://alozano.clas.uconn.edu/number-theory-and-geometry/)\nciteturn571search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Álvaro Lozano-Robledo ... In this page you can find a free version of my book “Number Theory and Geometry” which was published by the American Mathematical Society in 2019, as part of their Pure and Applied Undergraduate Mathematics Texts Series. ... The official page for the book is here: https://bookstore.ams.org/amstext-35\n\nDepartment of Mathematics\n\nÁlvaro Lozano-Robledo\n\nSearch this Site Search in https://alozano.clas.uconn.edu/>[Input: Search this site... ]Search\n\n# Number Theory and Geometry\n\nIn this page you can find a free version of my book “Number Theory and Geometry” which was published by the American Mathematical Society in 2019, as part of their Pure and Applied Undergraduate Mathematics Texts Series.\n\nSome notes:\n\n  * I would like to thank the AMS for allowing me to post a free version of the book here.\n  * Please note that the version posted here is not equivalent to the published version. The AMS put many hours into polishing and editing the manuscript before printing, so please consider supporting the AMS publishing by purchasing their books! The official page for the book is here: https://bookstore.ams.org/amstext-35 \n  * I keep a page with updates/comments/errata (which will hopefully be incorporated in a later version/edition of the book). Thanks to all who have contacted me with comments!\n\nHere is the free version! Enjoy:\n\nNUMBER THEORY AND GEOMETRY, by Álvaro Lozano-Robledo.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMATH 5020 - The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves | Álvaro Lozano-Robledo | Department of Mathematics | University of Connecticut (https://alozano.clas.uconn.edu/math5020-elliptic-curves/)\nciteturn571search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; †www.ams.org】”, in the Notices of the American Mathematical Society, Vol 64, Issue 3, March 2017, pp. ... Lozano-Robledo, “Elliptic Curves, Modular Forms, and their L-Functions” is another elementary introduction to elliptic curves. ... Milne, “Elliptic Curves,” published by Kea Books – this book is freely available at Milne’s website. ... 4  | Varieties, function fields, dimension (part 1), and\n### Videos\n\nHere is a list of video playlists that may be useful:\n\n  * Videos of lectures for this class.\n  * A mini-course on elliptic curves.\n  * Videos on elliptic curve cryptography.\n  * A mini-course on Galois representations.\n  * A list of videos intros to the LMFDB, Magma, and Sage/CoCalc.\n\n* * *\n\n### Homework\n\nHomework sets will be posted here periodically. The sets are a small selection of problems that should help students in understanding the theory, but of course there are many other problems available in Silverman’s book that are also a great resource for the learner. This is a topics class, so the homework sets are not mandatory. I’ll be happy to look at sets if you want to send me your solutions, but in general I expect that if you are interested in the material, you will be trying the problems on your own and reaching out to me when you need help or hints. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElliptic Curves, Modular Forms, and Their L-Functions (Student Mathematical Library - Ias/Park City Mathematical Subseries) - Alvaro Lozano-Robledo - 9780